{{also|fakír}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ar|en}} {{term|فقير|tr=faqīr||poor man|sc=Arab|lang=ar}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fəˈkiɹ/|/fɑˈkiɹ/|/ˈfeɪkəɹ/|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|faker}}
* {{rhymes|ɪə(ɹ)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|Islam|lang=en}} A [[faqir]].
# {{context|Hindu|lang=en}} An [[ascetic]] [[mendicant]], especially one who [[perform]]s [[feat]]s of [[endurance]] or apparent [[magic]].
#*{{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=16
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=The preposterous altruism too!{{...}}Resist not evil. It is an insane immolation of self—as bad intrinsically as '''fakirs''' stabbing themselves or anchorites warping their spines in caves scarcely large enough for a fair-sized dog.}}

